1
00:00:00,960 --> 00:00:05,160
It's an invisible force all around us.

2
00:00:05,161 --> 00:00:09,840
In the air and beneath the sea.

3
00:00:09,841 --> 00:00:15,160
It can cut through metal and tear a house down.

4
00:00:15,161 --> 00:00:18,160
It has a lot to do with weather.

5
00:00:18,161 --> 00:00:22,840
And particularly, it has a lot to do with wind.

6
00:00:22,841 --> 00:00:28,680
From a nuclear explosion to blowing up a balloon,

7
00:00:28,681 --> 00:00:31,780
you'll never feel pressure the same way again.

8
00:00:31,781 --> 00:00:35,320
This is Modern Marvel's Superpower Pressure.

9
00:00:48,660 --> 00:00:52,100
Pressure is hard at work all around us.

10
00:00:52,101 --> 00:00:55,000
An invisible force that makes things move faster,

11
00:00:55,001 --> 00:00:58,760
change consistency, or raise and lower temperature.

12
00:01:01,040 --> 00:01:04,080
Pressure is often hard to see, but it can be measured,

13
00:01:04,520 --> 00:01:07,620
whether it's in liquids, in solids, or in gases.

14
00:01:08,300 --> 00:01:11,820
Pressure is registered in pounds per square inch, or PSI.

15
00:01:12,840 --> 00:01:16,980
That's literally the weight of one pound pressing down on one square inch.

16
00:01:17,840 --> 00:01:20,880
Add weight, and the pressure goes up.

17
00:01:20,881 --> 00:01:25,920
The water that comes from your tap, that's about 35 PSI.

18
00:01:27,100 --> 00:01:31,340
The water shooting out of a fire hose, about 100 PSI.

19
00:01:38,880 --> 00:01:42,300
Nobody understands the power of PSI like firefighters.

20
00:01:44,080 --> 00:01:47,260
Engine 22 of the Danbury, Connecticut Fire Department

21
00:01:47,261 --> 00:01:50,180
is a state-of-the-art mobile firefighting platform

22
00:01:50,181 --> 00:01:55,180
designed to do one thing, generate flame-killing water pressure.

23
00:01:56,060 --> 00:01:56,820
Lots of it.

24
00:02:02,060 --> 00:02:05,240
Engines like these are technological marvels,

25
00:02:05,241 --> 00:02:08,300
capable of delivering more than 1,200 gallons per minute

26
00:02:08,301 --> 00:02:10,200
via multiple hose connections.

27
00:02:11,100 --> 00:02:14,980
That's because the engine's internal pump creates enormous pressure

28
00:02:14,981 --> 00:02:17,200
that can push water out at top speeds.

29
00:02:17,201 --> 00:02:25,180
engine 22 only carries 500 gallons so these high-pressure hoses are going to need more water

30
00:02:25,181 --> 00:02:37,720
fast in most fires that water comes from a fire hydrant a five-inch diameter supply

31
00:02:37,721 --> 00:02:46,180
hose locks into the town's water system the pressure is on and the hose springs to attention

32
00:02:47,200 --> 00:02:54,200
But municipal fire hydrants are unpredictable, delivering water at varying pressures.

33
00:02:54,201 --> 00:03:02,200
So the fire engine itself juggles the inflows and outflows, keeping the pressure constant.

34
00:03:02,201 --> 00:03:07,200
Tackling a fire from a ladder truck adds another layer of complexity.

35
00:03:07,201 --> 00:03:13,200
The engine has to generate additional PSI to compensate for its height over the ground.

36
00:03:13,201 --> 00:03:15,740
22 driver, 24, water's on the way.

37
00:03:18,040 --> 00:03:21,100
It's like pumping 1,000 gallons of high-pressure water

38
00:03:21,101 --> 00:03:22,800
up a steep hill every minute.

39
00:03:24,080 --> 00:03:26,540
You have big fire, you need big water.

40
00:03:27,680 --> 00:03:30,380
Firefighters can't handle this volume of water by hand.

41
00:03:31,040 --> 00:03:34,240
We could unsheathe all the shingles off this roof.

42
00:03:35,160 --> 00:03:39,320
You're putting 1,000 gallons of water out at 80 pounds per square inch.

43
00:03:39,321 --> 00:03:40,280
That's a lot of force.

44
00:03:40,281 --> 00:03:43,940
it's an awesome amount of power.

45
00:03:47,160 --> 00:03:49,800
Nothing is more critical to this high-pressure offense

46
00:03:49,801 --> 00:03:51,280
than a strong defense,

47
00:03:51,780 --> 00:03:53,360
and it comes from a fire hose,

48
00:03:54,040 --> 00:03:56,960
a flexible cylinder lined with synthetic rubber.

49
00:03:57,780 --> 00:03:59,900
This is a rubber extrusion machine.

50
00:04:00,080 --> 00:04:01,420
We're making a tube liner.

51
00:04:01,920 --> 00:04:04,500
So the rubber actually works really well in the fire hose

52
00:04:04,501 --> 00:04:06,880
because it enables the fire hose to dilate

53
00:04:06,881 --> 00:04:09,180
and swell and stretch under pressure.

54
00:04:09,181 --> 00:04:12,440
It also obviously provides sealant for the water to transfer.

55
00:04:13,200 --> 00:04:19,680
All-American hose in western Pennsylvania uses about two and a half tons of synthetic rubber every day.

56
00:04:21,740 --> 00:04:26,080
This liner will fit inside a sturdy outside covering made of tightly woven nylon.

57
00:04:28,440 --> 00:04:31,300
The weaving machine spins at 100 RPMs.

58
00:04:32,640 --> 00:04:37,060
The weave is so tight, the machine's output is only two feet per minute.

59
00:04:39,180 --> 00:04:44,680
After weaving, each jacket is manually inspected for any defects that could cause the hose

60
00:04:44,681 --> 00:04:49,940
to weaken and burst under firefighting pressures.

61
00:04:49,941 --> 00:04:54,360
The jacket and rubber liner come together on this 100-foot-long table.

62
00:04:54,361 --> 00:04:58,900
A fire hose is usually made of three sections.

63
00:04:58,901 --> 00:05:06,100
One is the inner rubber liner, which we insert into the inner jacket, and then we take that

64
00:05:06,101 --> 00:05:09,340
inner jacket liner assembly and we insert it into an outer jacket.

65
00:05:12,660 --> 00:05:15,780
That protects the hose whenever it's under pressure.

66
00:05:16,060 --> 00:05:18,360
It also protects it when it's under a fire condition.

67
00:05:20,060 --> 00:05:25,220
Pressure seals the deal with an 80 PSI hot steam treatment for 15 minutes.

68
00:05:27,920 --> 00:05:31,640
The steam expands the hose to operating size for the first time

69
00:05:31,641 --> 00:05:36,420
and activates an adhesive that binds the inner layers together.

70
00:05:37,900 --> 00:05:41,500
The steam heat and pressure make the fire hose even more rugged.

71
00:05:46,560 --> 00:05:50,740
With the addition of alloy couplings, the fire hose is almost finished.

72
00:05:53,440 --> 00:05:58,860
But first, it must endure a proof test, where it's subjected to 800 PSI.

73
00:05:59,860 --> 00:06:03,520
That's two times greater than the maximum that firefighters will ever need.

74
00:06:04,760 --> 00:06:05,460
Why?

75
00:06:05,461 --> 00:06:10,520
Because pressure can spike unexpectedly when hoses are twisted, crimped, or run over.

76
00:06:12,240 --> 00:06:17,260
Some hoses undergo an additional burst test, over 1,200 PSI.

77
00:06:17,760 --> 00:06:18,980
That's enough to break them open.

78
00:06:19,580 --> 00:06:24,840
But the way this hose is designed to burst along its seam, it could handle even that.

79
00:06:24,841 --> 00:06:29,340
It pumps up the hose to the burst pressure.

80
00:06:29,840 --> 00:06:34,460
And when the hose bursts, we want it to burst long-wise so it's safe like a banana.

81
00:06:34,980 --> 00:06:38,240
If it bursts in the cross-section, then it can be a loose cannon.

82
00:06:38,800 --> 00:06:40,440
You'd have hose flying all around.

83
00:06:42,140 --> 00:06:45,360
It takes more than 1,200 PSI to burst this hose,

84
00:06:45,860 --> 00:06:48,820
a key test of its ability to withstand enormous pressure.

85
00:06:52,260 --> 00:06:54,680
But even to those of us not battling fires,

86
00:06:54,840 --> 00:06:57,240
Water pressure plays an essential role.

87
00:06:58,940 --> 00:07:01,420
When you turn on the tap and water flows out,

88
00:07:01,780 --> 00:07:02,960
it's thanks to water pressure.

89
00:07:03,840 --> 00:07:07,180
But how does water pressure work, and why is it so reliable?

90
00:07:09,040 --> 00:07:12,280
As a liquid, all water naturally flows downhill,

91
00:07:12,800 --> 00:07:14,720
picking up power and pressure as it travels.

92
00:07:18,640 --> 00:07:21,320
It's the same basic way water gets to your house.

93
00:07:21,321 --> 00:07:25,160
A water tower is simply a large water reservoir,

94
00:07:25,161 --> 00:07:28,320
elevated, to generate pressure and propel water

95
00:07:28,321 --> 00:07:31,320
into your kitchen sink.

96
00:07:31,321 --> 00:07:34,320
Water towers use the most patient force I know,

97
00:07:34,321 --> 00:07:35,320
and that's gravity.

98
00:07:35,321 --> 00:07:38,320
All you have to do is rely on the mass of water

99
00:07:38,321 --> 00:07:41,320
to push it through the pipes, and for the most part,

100
00:07:41,321 --> 00:07:44,320
you're in pretty good shape.

101
00:07:44,321 --> 00:07:47,320
Your typical water tower holds more than a million gallons,

102
00:07:47,321 --> 00:07:50,320
but its height is its most important feature.

103
00:07:51,320 --> 00:07:57,540
For every two feet that water is raised off the ground, it contributes one more pound

104
00:07:57,541 --> 00:08:00,200
of pressure, or PSI, to the system.

105
00:08:00,201 --> 00:08:05,100
A water tower must be tall enough to push water through a network of pipes and into

106
00:08:05,101 --> 00:08:09,740
surrounding homes and businesses.

107
00:08:09,741 --> 00:08:17,120
Most cities aim to generate pressure between 50 and 100 PSI.

108
00:08:17,121 --> 00:08:21,300
Water towers are usually taken out of service for maintenance once a year.

109
00:08:21,301 --> 00:08:29,060
This gives us a rare opportunity to step inside its pressure-creating construction.

110
00:08:29,061 --> 00:08:34,340
This tank is over 90 feet in diameter, and it can hold a million and a half gallons.

111
00:08:34,341 --> 00:08:42,460
When the tank is full, there would be 35 feet of water above us.

112
00:08:42,461 --> 00:08:47,080
In Loudoun County, Virginia, Coldwell Tanks constructed the country's biggest water tower

113
00:08:47,081 --> 00:08:50,340
site.

114
00:08:50,341 --> 00:08:56,580
These two massive 3 million gallon towers are half steel plate and half reinforced concrete.

115
00:08:56,581 --> 00:09:02,440
The pedestal has a diameter of 64 feet and 12 inch thick walls all the way around.

116
00:09:02,441 --> 00:09:08,440
The pedestal height is on the order of 110 feet, including the dome that is on top of

117
00:09:08,441 --> 00:09:12,140
the pedestal.

118
00:09:12,141 --> 00:09:18,040
The 500 ton tank is hoisted up to the pedestal's dome and locked into position.

119
00:09:18,041 --> 00:09:25,040
This 116-foot diameter tank delivers 50 pounds of pressure into the local water system, serving

120
00:09:25,041 --> 00:09:29,100
15,000 households.

121
00:09:29,101 --> 00:09:32,200
But what happens to your water pressure if you live in a building that's taller than

122
00:09:32,201 --> 00:09:35,140
the highest water tower?

123
00:09:35,141 --> 00:09:39,320
That's a problem that New York City first faced more than 100 years ago, when it began

124
00:09:39,321 --> 00:09:41,140
to grow taller.

125
00:09:41,141 --> 00:09:44,640
There wasn't nearly enough water pressure for the topmost floors.

126
00:09:44,641 --> 00:09:47,420
The solution became an icon.

127
00:09:47,421 --> 00:09:49,920
A water tank for every building over 80 feet.

128
00:09:50,800 --> 00:09:54,080
Water tanks are old school, but they still work.

129
00:09:54,780 --> 00:09:57,580
Water tanks like these are replaced every 25 years.

130
00:10:02,180 --> 00:10:04,580
The job has to be completed in less than a day.

131
00:10:05,560 --> 00:10:08,420
A challenge American pipe and tank is ready to take on.

132
00:10:09,680 --> 00:10:12,880
While this tank is empty, this building has no fire protection.

133
00:10:13,440 --> 00:10:16,320
So it's pretty critical that gets back online as soon as possible.

134
00:10:17,420 --> 00:10:28,740
The team spends the first two hours dismantling the old water tank.

135
00:10:28,741 --> 00:10:30,000
Watch it!

136
00:10:35,640 --> 00:10:39,640
Next, they assemble a barrel large enough to supply this nine-story building.

137
00:10:41,340 --> 00:10:43,020
5,000 gallons should do it.

138
00:10:43,021 --> 00:10:52,340
First, the team erects a circle of staves, and then tightens them into position by a

139
00:10:52,341 --> 00:10:57,640
series of hoops.

140
00:10:57,641 --> 00:11:02,180
All of this while dangling more than 100 feet over the pavement.

141
00:11:02,181 --> 00:11:06,820
In New York City, the majority of old and new water tanks are made of wood.

142
00:11:06,821 --> 00:11:10,700
Wood is a great material for an outdoor water tank.

143
00:11:10,701 --> 00:11:12,380
Wood is a natural product.

144
00:11:12,381 --> 00:11:14,880
It breathes, it does very well outdoors.

145
00:11:18,720 --> 00:11:21,180
When you actually look at this tank from the top,

146
00:11:21,181 --> 00:11:23,860
you will look down and you will see a true circle.

147
00:11:23,861 --> 00:11:28,060
Perfectly milled and installed like a big jigsaw puzzle.

148
00:11:28,061 --> 00:11:30,140
Like all water towers, once completed,

149
00:11:30,141 --> 00:11:32,040
the tank will require very little attention

150
00:11:32,041 --> 00:11:34,560
to do its pressure-generating job.

151
00:11:34,561 --> 00:11:38,840
The water tank is a very maintenance-free type of system.

152
00:11:38,841 --> 00:11:41,140
A small pump in the basement fills the tank

153
00:11:41,141 --> 00:11:42,640
and gravity just does the rest.

154
00:11:44,840 --> 00:11:47,500
While New Yorkers are proud of their rooftop water tanks,

155
00:11:48,140 --> 00:11:50,300
some landmarks prefer to keep them out of sight.

156
00:11:52,120 --> 00:11:55,140
The Empire State Building is one of the first skyscrapers

157
00:11:55,141 --> 00:11:56,480
to hide its water on top.

158
00:11:58,680 --> 00:12:00,140
So even if you don't see them,

159
00:12:00,640 --> 00:12:02,740
water towers and rooftop tanks are up there,

160
00:12:03,940 --> 00:12:06,220
providing constant water and steady pressure.

161
00:12:06,221 --> 00:12:12,020
But what happens when pressure goes out of control?

162
00:12:15,960 --> 00:12:18,800
Countless aspects of our everyday lives are under pressure.

163
00:12:19,680 --> 00:12:23,580
We harness water pressure and store it high in the air until we need it.

164
00:12:25,200 --> 00:12:28,220
We bottle up air pressure and hold it in aerosol cans.

165
00:12:29,740 --> 00:12:34,080
It may be everywhere, but pressure is not always under our control.

166
00:12:36,220 --> 00:12:39,500
Explosions.

167
00:12:46,500 --> 00:12:49,100
An explosion causes rapid expansion,

168
00:12:49,540 --> 00:12:52,780
which gives birth to an unpredictable pressure wave

169
00:12:52,781 --> 00:12:54,780
that can travel over long distances.

170
00:13:00,560 --> 00:13:02,360
But what's really going on here?

171
00:13:03,860 --> 00:13:06,080
Imagine a pebble dropping into a pond.

172
00:13:06,220 --> 00:13:09,220
The ripples move out at a steady pace.

173
00:13:09,221 --> 00:13:16,220
An explosion is something like this, but in the open air, and not at all steady.

174
00:13:16,221 --> 00:13:21,220
Shock waves from an explosion can only move as fast as the speed of sound, so they bunch up.

175
00:13:21,221 --> 00:13:27,220
And that's what creates a wallop-packing pressure wave.

176
00:13:27,221 --> 00:13:31,220
But pressurized gases aren't always destructive.

177
00:13:31,221 --> 00:13:38,280
On a much smaller scale, we even bottle air's explosive pressure and enjoy it, bubble by bubble.

178
00:13:40,160 --> 00:13:43,740
Avery's Beverages has been putting the pop in soda pop for more than a century.

179
00:13:44,320 --> 00:13:48,320
If you want to see what 300 pounds of pressure is like, I can open a valve here.

180
00:13:48,400 --> 00:13:50,240
It's pretty loud, though I'll warn you ahead of time.

181
00:13:56,160 --> 00:13:59,780
Rob Metz is the mastermind who keeps these decades-old machines running.

182
00:13:59,781 --> 00:14:04,620
We're an old-fashioned soda company, and it's, as you can see, handmade.

183
00:14:05,740 --> 00:14:09,820
And we still make our soda the way it was done years ago.

184
00:14:12,460 --> 00:14:15,080
That means first making a batch of flavored syrup,

185
00:14:16,100 --> 00:14:18,560
and then adding the other half of the recipe.

186
00:14:20,460 --> 00:14:21,360
The fizz.

187
00:14:22,260 --> 00:14:25,940
The bubbles in a soft drink are carbon dioxide, or CO2.

188
00:14:25,941 --> 00:14:29,860
That's why we call the process of putting the bubbles in carbonation.

189
00:14:31,360 --> 00:14:33,220
Pressure plays a role in every step.

190
00:14:34,800 --> 00:14:40,660
Avery stores their CO2 in a liquid state under 300 pounds of pressure at 40 degrees below zero.

191
00:14:42,260 --> 00:14:45,720
This is where we control the amount of pressure going into the carbonator,

192
00:14:45,920 --> 00:14:48,120
and this is where we adjust it for the different flavors.

193
00:14:48,121 --> 00:14:51,900
For fruity flavor, we're going to have it running around 60 pounds per square inch.

194
00:14:51,901 --> 00:14:55,900
for a higher carbonated mixer or a cola or a ginger ale,

195
00:14:55,940 --> 00:14:58,640
we're going to run it at about 80 pounds per square inch.

196
00:14:59,820 --> 00:15:01,560
Higher pressure means more bubbles.

197
00:15:02,500 --> 00:15:03,400
And you heard right.

198
00:15:04,600 --> 00:15:07,460
Colas contain more bubbles than fruit-flavored sodas.

199
00:15:08,260 --> 00:15:10,620
That's simply because we like our colas much fizzier

200
00:15:10,621 --> 00:15:11,820
than other carbonated beverages.

201
00:15:13,940 --> 00:15:16,380
So how does CO2 find its way into water?

202
00:15:17,340 --> 00:15:20,620
Coming in the top here is our water line.

203
00:15:20,621 --> 00:15:24,060
and in the side here is where our carbon dioxide comes.

204
00:15:24,960 --> 00:15:27,760
Normally, you can't see the moment when the fizz goes in.

205
00:15:28,400 --> 00:15:29,780
Here, inside the carbonator,

206
00:15:30,260 --> 00:15:32,740
pressurized carbon dioxide is forced into the water,

207
00:15:33,260 --> 00:15:36,580
and a unique reaction occurs between the water and the gas.

208
00:15:37,200 --> 00:15:40,440
Carbon dioxide is special because it dissolves in the water.

209
00:15:41,860 --> 00:15:46,000
You can get a lot more CO2 into a bottle like this one

210
00:15:46,001 --> 00:15:48,660
than you can nitrogen or oxygen,

211
00:15:48,661 --> 00:15:51,320
and it's because the CO2 is more soluble.

212
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:54,520
By the time it's done, this water will have absorbed

213
00:15:54,521 --> 00:15:56,980
three times its own volume in carbon dioxide.

214
00:15:58,080 --> 00:15:59,280
That's a lot of gas.

215
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:03,300
And the higher the pressure, the faster the gas dissolves into the water.

216
00:16:05,940 --> 00:16:08,740
The soda finally comes together at the bottling station,

217
00:16:09,640 --> 00:16:12,420
where the flavored syrup gets dispensed

218
00:16:12,421 --> 00:16:14,640
and mixed with the carbonated water under pressure.

219
00:16:18,660 --> 00:16:23,660
Then the capper seals it off and traps the CO2.

220
00:16:23,661 --> 00:16:29,660
When they get down here, the syrup and the carbonated water aren't mixed.

221
00:16:29,661 --> 00:16:32,660
So every bottle is going to be inverted three times.

222
00:16:32,661 --> 00:16:34,660
One, two, three.

223
00:16:34,661 --> 00:16:36,660
And then they're mixed to stay mixed.

224
00:16:36,661 --> 00:16:39,660
So that's what's happening over here.

225
00:16:39,661 --> 00:16:42,660
Now it's time to sit back and enjoy.

226
00:16:42,661 --> 00:16:45,660
Just don't shake things up.

227
00:16:45,661 --> 00:16:52,660
Soda pop is by no means the only beverage in which we find CO2 bubbles.

228
00:16:52,661 --> 00:16:55,660
A celebratory champagne pop?

229
00:16:55,661 --> 00:16:58,660
That's the result of CO2 escaping the bottle.

230
00:16:58,661 --> 00:17:04,660
Shake the champagne, and you excite the gas, creating a surge of bubbles and pressure.

231
00:17:04,661 --> 00:17:08,660
Even agitated, much of the CO2 remains.

232
00:17:08,661 --> 00:17:11,660
There's still plenty of fizz to be enjoyed.

233
00:17:11,661 --> 00:17:18,660
But what if nearly every single CO2 bubble in a bottle is forced out?

234
00:17:18,661 --> 00:17:24,520
All at once?

235
00:17:24,521 --> 00:17:28,100
We don't know if history records the first person who ever tried this.

236
00:17:28,101 --> 00:17:33,000
But whoever it is, they ought to get the Nobel Prize for fun.

237
00:17:33,001 --> 00:17:34,760
It's what you're seeing here.

238
00:17:34,761 --> 00:17:38,600
The ultimate example of homemade explosive pressure.

239
00:17:38,601 --> 00:17:41,940
It's now the infamous Coke Mentos experiment.

240
00:17:41,941 --> 00:17:44,680
A roll of candy is dropped into a bottle of cola,

241
00:17:44,681 --> 00:17:48,600
and the pressure release is downright explosive.

242
00:17:48,601 --> 00:17:51,600
How does it work?

243
00:17:51,601 --> 00:17:54,780
Most scientists believe that the unusual surface of the candy

244
00:17:54,781 --> 00:17:57,980
spurs a rapid chain reaction of bubble formation,

245
00:17:57,981 --> 00:18:01,720
in which each new bubble creates even more bubbles

246
00:18:01,721 --> 00:18:05,540
and an instant release of all the pressure

247
00:18:05,541 --> 00:18:09,240
until the bottle is out of gas.

248
00:18:09,241 --> 00:18:12,340
So the explosive broth is all the carbon dioxide

249
00:18:12,341 --> 00:18:13,740
leaving the bottle at once.

250
00:18:16,660 --> 00:18:19,520
Like an explosion, there's lots of pressure,

251
00:18:19,521 --> 00:18:22,580
but it's released at a much slower rate.

252
00:18:22,581 --> 00:18:26,240
No pressure wave, no damage.

253
00:18:26,241 --> 00:18:28,100
Just one big mess.

254
00:18:28,101 --> 00:18:34,640
Pressure kicks off many of our days.

255
00:18:34,641 --> 00:18:38,340
It's the driving force behind another foamy mess.

256
00:18:38,341 --> 00:18:43,940
Only when this one blows up, you put it on your face.

257
00:18:43,941 --> 00:18:53,160
When Barbasol was invented in 1919,

258
00:18:53,161 --> 00:18:55,260
it was the world's first shaving cream.

259
00:18:55,261 --> 00:18:59,780
But it didn't become the fluffy stuff we know today until the 1950s,

260
00:18:59,781 --> 00:19:02,960
with the advent of the pressurized aerosol can.

261
00:19:03,940 --> 00:19:06,740
Before shaving cream, men had to lather up with soap.

262
00:19:08,040 --> 00:19:12,140
Shaving methods have evolved since then, but the materials, not so much.

263
00:19:14,460 --> 00:19:20,400
A visit to a Barbasol plant in Ohio reveals that shaving creams still share a key ingredient with soap,

264
00:19:20,960 --> 00:19:21,660
stearic acid.

265
00:19:21,661 --> 00:19:29,540
It starts as flakes, but it slowly transforms into a liquid as it heats to 150 degrees.

266
00:19:31,100 --> 00:19:38,480
Emulsifiers, sudsing agents, and filtered water are combined with stearic acid in massive 3,000-gallon mixing tanks.

267
00:19:39,600 --> 00:19:41,660
The pressure is yet to come.

268
00:19:43,820 --> 00:19:47,400
Over 10,000 empty cans enter the production line every hour.

269
00:19:47,401 --> 00:19:54,400
A 12-head filler processes 180 of them a minute.

270
00:19:54,401 --> 00:20:01,560
Well, the shaving cream at this stage pretty much looks like, I would compare it to skimmed

271
00:20:01,561 --> 00:20:02,560
milk.

272
00:20:02,561 --> 00:20:04,160
Fairly watery and cloudy.

273
00:20:04,161 --> 00:20:13,480
It's not until the propellant goes in the can that it gets the foamy, rich texture.

274
00:20:13,481 --> 00:20:17,367
valves are crimped to the top of each can, they're ready to receive pressure.

275
00:20:17,368 --> 00:20:18,068
But for

276
00:20:18,069 --> 00:20:23,544
that step, the entire production line makes a detour out of the factory building.

277
00:20:23,545 --> 00:20:24,245
The

278
00:20:24,246 --> 00:20:27,964
pressure is injected in a gas house that's separate from the factory.

279
00:20:27,965 --> 00:20:29,000
Barbasol uses two

280
00:20:29,001 --> 00:20:32,370
gases as propellants, propane and isobutane.

281
00:20:32,371 --> 00:20:35,740
Both are potentially explosive, which is why

282
00:20:35,741 --> 00:20:38,540
this process is done outside the factory walls.

283
00:20:38,541 --> 00:20:43,820
So if there were a spark present, the roof would actually get blown off the building,

284
00:20:44,200 --> 00:20:47,600
and this room would get foamed to suppress the fire.

285
00:20:48,700 --> 00:20:52,700
The amount of propellant in a single can is not enough for a consumer to worry about.

286
00:20:54,200 --> 00:20:57,600
Once the cans leave this building, they are under pressure.

287
00:20:59,800 --> 00:21:02,780
The cans return to the factory under 80 PSI.

288
00:21:03,240 --> 00:21:06,340
Then they run through a hot bath to ensure that there are no leaks.

289
00:21:08,540 --> 00:21:14,940
The heat increases pressure to over 100 psi, simulating conditions the cans might encounter during shipping.

290
00:21:17,180 --> 00:21:22,280
But how does shaving cream go from the consistency of skim milk to this?

291
00:21:23,980 --> 00:21:26,900
You have to understand what's going on inside the can.

292
00:21:27,560 --> 00:21:29,920
The propellant gas is mixed with a soapy solution.

293
00:21:31,220 --> 00:21:33,380
Under pressure, they become a single liquid.

294
00:21:33,381 --> 00:21:40,380
When the valve opens, the pressure pushes the liquid out and foamy cream emerges.

295
00:21:40,381 --> 00:21:48,160
The material, the propellant, is above its boiling point at room temperature.

296
00:21:48,161 --> 00:21:55,020
As a result, when I open the valve, I release the pressure and the propellant boils.

297
00:21:55,021 --> 00:21:59,240
That small amount of liquid turns into a huge amount of gas, and that's the way you're

298
00:21:59,241 --> 00:22:04,020
able to get a large amount of foam into a small can of shaving cream.

299
00:22:05,060 --> 00:22:09,740
So inside a can of shaving cream, pressure reveals what a versatile force it can be.

300
00:22:13,900 --> 00:22:17,020
But what if you need a little more power than the pressure found in a can?

301
00:22:19,280 --> 00:22:26,180
Once you start looking for it, pressure is just about everywhere, from the common to

302
00:22:26,181 --> 00:22:26,881
the critical.

303
00:22:26,882 --> 00:22:29,760
It plays a role in nearly every job.

304
00:22:32,320 --> 00:22:35,320
Cut something, and it's pressure that does the work.

305
00:22:37,800 --> 00:22:40,600
It doesn't matter what's in your hand or what you're dividing.

306
00:22:43,700 --> 00:22:46,920
The best cutter in the world employs blistering pressure.

307
00:22:47,880 --> 00:22:50,440
Yet it's cutting with nothing more than water.

308
00:22:51,680 --> 00:22:55,460
It's a great demonstration of the incredible power of liquids under pressure.

309
00:22:56,620 --> 00:22:58,720
But where does all that force come from?

310
00:23:00,520 --> 00:23:05,220
JetEdge Water Jet Systems has built a machine that puts water under massive pressure.

311
00:23:05,700 --> 00:23:09,400
It's called, appropriately enough, a water intensifier system.

312
00:23:10,680 --> 00:23:15,360
A hydraulic pump turns at 100 horsepower, generating 3,000 psi.

313
00:23:16,320 --> 00:23:22,880
This pressure is transferred to a cylinder that uses a piston to put 75,000 pounds of pressure on water.

314
00:23:22,881 --> 00:23:29,380
If 75,000 pounds of pressure per square inch sounds like a lot, it is.

315
00:23:30,240 --> 00:23:30,980
Consider this.

316
00:23:31,580 --> 00:23:35,380
Nuclear subs can't survive deeper than 2,400 feet in the ocean,

317
00:23:36,080 --> 00:23:39,320
where the pressure is little more than 1,000 psi.

318
00:23:46,700 --> 00:23:51,020
Water exits the nozzle at Mach 2, roughly 2,000 miles an hour.

319
00:23:51,021 --> 00:23:54,520
That's faster than a bullet exits a gun.

320
00:23:57,400 --> 00:24:01,420
A jet of pressurized water has a lot in common with another high-tech tool.

321
00:24:02,480 --> 00:24:07,560
The ability to concentrate this stream of water into a very small area,

322
00:24:08,020 --> 00:24:13,680
it's kind of like using water as a laser, because that's what a laser beam also does as well.

323
00:24:14,160 --> 00:24:17,180
The precision of a water cutter is unparalleled.

324
00:24:18,080 --> 00:24:21,860
The water jet blasts through a sheet of carbon fiber as if it were butter,

325
00:24:22,260 --> 00:24:26,100
and it will do the same to 12-inch steel or titanium.

326
00:24:27,580 --> 00:24:30,020
First of all, the water acts as a lubricant,

327
00:24:30,300 --> 00:24:34,300
and second, it also acts as a cooling agent to take some of that heat away.

328
00:24:34,680 --> 00:24:37,940
Unlike a water jet, other cutting devices generate heat,

329
00:24:38,320 --> 00:24:41,320
which can warp the material or make it brittle.

330
00:24:45,160 --> 00:24:46,980
A water jet is unstoppable.

331
00:24:47,180 --> 00:24:51,920
In fact, if it weren't for a two-foot deep bath of water underneath the material being cut,

332
00:24:52,460 --> 00:24:56,100
the water jet would be carving holes in the factory floor.

333
00:24:56,700 --> 00:24:58,100
Water can cut a number of things.

334
00:24:58,500 --> 00:25:01,140
You can cut sheet metal, foam, rubber.

335
00:25:01,680 --> 00:25:05,820
But if you want to cut something really thick, like, say, six inches of titanium,

336
00:25:06,160 --> 00:25:08,280
then you've got to add garnet abrasive.

337
00:25:08,800 --> 00:25:12,600
And that is this stuff right here.

338
00:25:13,240 --> 00:25:16,580
The abrasive adds erosive power to the pressurized water

339
00:25:16,581 --> 00:25:17,960
and accelerates the cutting.

340
00:25:21,100 --> 00:25:23,720
Water jet cutting has been a boon to industries,

341
00:25:23,980 --> 00:25:24,820
like auto racing.

342
00:25:25,960 --> 00:25:28,340
Michael Waltrip Racing builds multiple teams

343
00:25:28,341 --> 00:25:29,380
on the NASCAR circuit.

344
00:25:30,460 --> 00:25:32,120
They build dozens of cars

345
00:25:32,121 --> 00:25:34,220
in their North Carolina facility every year.

346
00:25:37,920 --> 00:25:40,300
Custom parts cut by high-pressure water

347
00:25:40,301 --> 00:25:41,920
are used every step of the way.

348
00:25:42,920 --> 00:25:44,880
Removing the engine reveals all the parts

349
00:25:44,881 --> 00:25:45,940
cut by the water jet.

350
00:25:46,580 --> 00:25:48,040
Here we have a bare chassis.

351
00:25:48,920 --> 00:25:52,520
We can see a large number of components that we cut from the water jet,

352
00:25:52,920 --> 00:25:56,580
from our engine mounts here and on the back of the engine.

353
00:25:57,620 --> 00:26:00,220
Suspension pickup parts are all cut from the water jet.

354
00:26:00,860 --> 00:26:04,840
We've got steering arm braces where the steering connects is all cut on the water jet.

355
00:26:06,280 --> 00:26:08,760
The water jet cutter shapes the parts quickly

356
00:26:08,761 --> 00:26:11,140
and to an engineer's precise specifications.

357
00:26:11,141 --> 00:26:16,800
When you're traveling at over 200 miles per hour,

358
00:26:17,320 --> 00:26:19,200
or failure can be a matter of life and death,

359
00:26:20,220 --> 00:26:23,100
you want to know that every part works perfectly.

360
00:26:23,800 --> 00:26:25,580
By machining parts flawlessly,

361
00:26:26,000 --> 00:26:28,080
a water jet cutter makes racing safer.

362
00:26:31,300 --> 00:26:33,160
Pressurized water is ideal for cutting,

363
00:26:34,000 --> 00:26:36,140
and pressurized air can be just as useful.

364
00:26:37,360 --> 00:26:39,080
Although it lacks the weight of water,

365
00:26:39,400 --> 00:26:40,800
air pressure can turn into something

366
00:26:40,801 --> 00:26:45,500
that will blow you away with a sandblast.

367
00:26:46,800 --> 00:26:49,140
These machines clean the toughest surfaces,

368
00:26:49,680 --> 00:26:51,300
all powered by compressed air.

369
00:26:52,900 --> 00:26:55,840
Nothing beats a sandblaster at scouring paint or grit

370
00:26:55,841 --> 00:26:57,360
and grime from old parts.

371
00:26:58,860 --> 00:27:00,440
And just like with a water jet,

372
00:27:00,740 --> 00:27:03,160
the secret ingredient is another gritty abrasive

373
00:27:03,161 --> 00:27:04,240
that's driven by pressure.

374
00:27:04,920 --> 00:27:06,260
With pressurized media,

375
00:27:06,340 --> 00:27:07,580
you don't have to go anything over that.

376
00:27:07,581 --> 00:27:10,020
I normally recommend 90 to 100 for blasting.

377
00:27:10,800 --> 00:27:14,720
When it gets fully decompressed, that handle will drop, and that tells you you're ready to fill.

378
00:27:15,600 --> 00:27:16,540
Now you're ready to fill.

379
00:27:17,280 --> 00:27:25,740
If a 100 PSI sandblaster sounds lame compared to the pressures of a 75,000 PSI water jet cutter, it's not.

380
00:27:26,600 --> 00:27:31,260
At 100 PSI, a sandblaster will strip naked skin to the bone.

381
00:27:32,920 --> 00:27:39,580
Bad Boy Blasters manufactures custom sandblasters, and every single one relies on pressurized air.

382
00:27:40,800 --> 00:27:44,440
But blasting with abrasives is a dirty, dusty job

383
00:27:44,441 --> 00:27:47,840
and can require a lot of cleanup.

384
00:27:47,841 --> 00:27:50,400
They've invented a vacuum that sucks away the abrasives

385
00:27:50,401 --> 00:27:53,960
and paint as fast as it can be blasted.

386
00:27:53,961 --> 00:27:57,800
The paint dust stays in the air filter.

387
00:27:57,801 --> 00:27:58,600
Turn on the vacuum.

388
00:28:03,800 --> 00:28:07,960
You can see at the 120 PSI, this is running a 40-60 grit.

389
00:28:07,961 --> 00:28:10,320
how nice of a, that's a good paintable service.

390
00:28:11,640 --> 00:28:15,420
A self-contained blast cabinet made of welded sheet metal

391
00:28:15,421 --> 00:28:17,420
makes sandblasting even less messy.

392
00:28:19,980 --> 00:28:21,520
Sandblasters are highly efficient,

393
00:28:22,000 --> 00:28:24,040
yet despite the name, they don't use sand.

394
00:28:26,100 --> 00:28:29,820
Sand can cause silicosis, a potentially fatal lung disease.

395
00:28:31,600 --> 00:28:35,840
Most industrial sandblasters like these now use aluminum oxide,

396
00:28:35,841 --> 00:28:38,640
the same material commonly found on sandpaper.

397
00:28:39,760 --> 00:28:42,600
Aluminum oxide can survive multiple runs through a blaster.

398
00:28:43,920 --> 00:28:45,060
I can blast anything.

399
00:28:45,180 --> 00:28:47,300
I got people doing anything from blasting glass,

400
00:28:47,480 --> 00:28:49,280
doing designs, metal parts,

401
00:28:49,720 --> 00:28:51,320
a lot of aircraft industry work.

402
00:28:52,640 --> 00:28:54,660
It may not pack the punch of a water jet,

403
00:28:55,380 --> 00:28:57,520
but a blast of air pressure is all it takes

404
00:28:57,521 --> 00:29:00,560
to turn an old hunk of metal into something shiny and new.

405
00:29:00,561 --> 00:29:07,000
Pressure is everywhere.

406
00:29:08,260 --> 00:29:09,280
Just look up.

407
00:29:11,140 --> 00:29:14,540
The atmosphere rises more than 20 miles over our heads.

408
00:29:15,780 --> 00:29:17,560
And all that air has weight.

409
00:29:18,400 --> 00:29:20,040
A very precise weight, in fact.

410
00:29:20,580 --> 00:29:22,220
14.7 pounds.

411
00:29:24,140 --> 00:29:27,400
That's how much is pressing down on those of us who live at sea level.

412
00:29:27,620 --> 00:29:28,360
All day.

413
00:29:28,760 --> 00:29:29,740
And we hardly notice.

414
00:29:30,560 --> 00:29:33,400
But there's something that doesn't get our attention.

415
00:29:34,200 --> 00:29:36,020
Atmospheric pressure lows and highs.

416
00:29:36,420 --> 00:29:38,280
We know it as weather.

417
00:29:41,220 --> 00:29:43,420
Air pressure has a lot to do with weather,

418
00:29:43,680 --> 00:29:45,620
and particularly it has a lot to do with wind.

419
00:29:46,360 --> 00:29:47,580
Check out any weather map.

420
00:29:48,200 --> 00:29:51,400
The highs and lows are air systems seeking to equalize pressure.

421
00:29:52,060 --> 00:29:55,380
Wind blows from areas of high pressure into those with low pressure.

422
00:29:57,080 --> 00:30:00,340
If you want to think of it that way, the more steep is the drop-off.

423
00:30:00,560 --> 00:30:03,660
From high pressure to low pressure, the more severe is the weather.

424
00:30:05,180 --> 00:30:09,400
Up there, it's a maelstrom of varying pressures, temperatures, and humidities.

425
00:30:10,120 --> 00:30:13,300
And in extreme cases, it turns deadly.

426
00:30:18,600 --> 00:30:23,880
But air doesn't exert nearly as much pressure as the other substance that rings our planet, water.

427
00:30:25,620 --> 00:30:30,440
30 feet of seawater creates the same amount of pressure as 20 miles of air.

428
00:30:30,560 --> 00:30:34,400
Some of the most destructive forces humans must overcome

429
00:30:34,500 --> 00:30:35,600
are in the deep sea.

430
00:30:37,500 --> 00:30:39,900
At just 100 feet, water pressure

431
00:30:40,000 --> 00:30:41,700
crushes an empty water bottle.

432
00:30:41,800 --> 00:30:45,500
That's how much the air inside it has compressed.

433
00:30:45,600 --> 00:30:48,020
Of course, you wouldn't be seeing images like these at all

434
00:30:48,120 --> 00:30:51,520
if it weren't for divers with video cameras.

435
00:30:51,620 --> 00:30:54,520
We're under a lot of pressure, time pressure,

436
00:30:54,620 --> 00:30:57,320
water pressure, to do a good job.

437
00:30:57,420 --> 00:30:59,260
Highly trained underwater cameramen

438
00:30:59,261 --> 00:31:04,660
where cameramen like Bob Cranston put their bodies on the line every time they dive.

439
00:31:04,661 --> 00:31:09,660
And the video gear they take down with them is worth tens of thousands of dollars.

440
00:31:09,661 --> 00:31:13,660
For divers, crushing pressures are the enemy.

441
00:31:13,661 --> 00:31:19,440
And what we're feeling when we go in the water is the weight of the water over our head.

442
00:31:19,441 --> 00:31:24,860
The deeper we go, the more pressure there is.

443
00:31:24,861 --> 00:31:26,860
Waterproofing alone won't do it.

444
00:31:26,861 --> 00:31:31,460
The only way you can take a camera underwater is using a pressure-resistant shell, called

445
00:31:31,461 --> 00:31:33,540
an underwater housing.

446
00:31:33,541 --> 00:31:39,520
Gates Underwater Products designs housing for all types of cameras.

447
00:31:39,521 --> 00:31:42,020
The process involves a series of trade-offs.

448
00:31:42,021 --> 00:31:46,140
The shell needs to be big enough to fit the camera, yet cylindrical in shape, to resist

449
00:31:46,141 --> 00:31:47,140
pressure.

450
00:31:47,141 --> 00:31:53,540
The fewer the parts, the fewer things that can go wrong.

451
00:31:53,541 --> 00:32:01,520
The main section of the housing emerges from a 65 and a half pound block of solid aluminum.

452
00:32:01,521 --> 00:32:11,400
Machines remove 88% of the metal, leaving a hollow eight pound cylinder.

453
00:32:11,401 --> 00:32:15,620
Then it's on to the tumbler, which polishes away any sharp edges with synthetic rocks

454
00:32:15,621 --> 00:32:20,420
and water.

455
00:32:20,421 --> 00:32:22,960
But even if all parts are perfectly machined,

456
00:32:22,961 --> 00:32:26,960
a seal can always fail due to human error.

457
00:32:26,961 --> 00:32:28,960
The user has to do a visual inspection

458
00:32:28,961 --> 00:32:31,560
to make sure there is nothing on the O-ring

459
00:32:31,561 --> 00:32:33,040
that can compromise it.

460
00:32:33,041 --> 00:32:36,260
Something as small as, say, a human hair

461
00:32:36,261 --> 00:32:40,740
laying across the O-ring can allow water to pass at that point.

462
00:32:40,741 --> 00:32:44,980
Large silicone O-rings seal big openings against pressure.

463
00:32:44,981 --> 00:32:47,180
And tiny sleeves, called glands,

464
00:32:47,181 --> 00:32:50,060
employ a double set of seals to maintain pressure

465
00:32:50,061 --> 00:32:53,780
the knobs and shafts that control the camera.

466
00:32:53,781 --> 00:32:57,200
As the last step before plunging into the crushing ocean,

467
00:32:57,201 --> 00:32:59,420
workers test the housing's ability to survive.

468
00:33:03,380 --> 00:33:06,120
First, they test for normal pressure

469
00:33:06,121 --> 00:33:08,000
by sucking all the air out of the housing.

470
00:33:12,180 --> 00:33:14,860
Now we wait to see if there's any changes in the numbers

471
00:33:14,861 --> 00:33:17,040
on the pressure gauge.

472
00:33:17,041 --> 00:33:18,860
If normal air pressure can't find its way

473
00:33:18,861 --> 00:33:20,600
into the vacuum and the housing.

474
00:33:20,601 --> 00:33:23,240
Then it's ready for a much more serious pressure test.

475
00:33:25,160 --> 00:33:26,180
The housing didn't lose any pressure,

476
00:33:26,181 --> 00:33:27,180
it's perfectly sealed.

477
00:33:33,700 --> 00:33:36,900
But how will it do when the pressure is multiplied?

478
00:33:36,901 --> 00:33:39,240
So now I'm adding pressure to the tank,

479
00:33:39,241 --> 00:33:41,000
which will bring us up to at least 100 psi,

480
00:33:42,880 --> 00:33:45,500
and simulate our dive to about 225 psi.

481
00:33:48,860 --> 00:33:55,860
Thorough leak testing requires putting every moving part to the test.

482
00:33:55,861 --> 00:34:01,860
I'm looking for any water that might be in any control shaft or around any sealing areas.

483
00:34:01,861 --> 00:34:08,860
And we'll actually torque on some of the controls to make sure that we have nothing in and around the control glands themselves.

484
00:34:08,861 --> 00:34:15,860
Checking around the port area. This one passed the test.

485
00:34:15,861 --> 00:34:20,860
Down here, minimal visibility makes gathering images even more challenging.

486
00:34:20,861 --> 00:34:30,860
Gates' camera housings can perform in waters as deep as 450 feet, where the pressure is more than 215 PSI,

487
00:34:30,861 --> 00:34:36,860
about 15 times greater than it is on the surface.

488
00:34:36,861 --> 00:34:45,440
Even at 100 feet, the PSI is four times greater than at the surface,

489
00:34:45,441 --> 00:34:49,620
a crushing force that also challenges a diver's body.

490
00:34:50,760 --> 00:34:54,020
The ocean applies pressure on all air spaces.

491
00:34:54,500 --> 00:34:57,820
Our lungs have air inside of it, our masks, our ears.

492
00:34:58,160 --> 00:35:02,220
All of these areas have to be equalized in our body.

493
00:35:02,600 --> 00:35:06,360
Otherwise, we feel squeeze and pain, and it's a bad thing.

494
00:35:06,860 --> 00:35:13,260
Even the air coursing through a diver's bloodstream changes with the pressure.

495
00:35:13,261 --> 00:35:19,740
This is one of diving's greatest hazards.

496
00:35:19,741 --> 00:35:26,240
Every minute a diver spends underwater, his blood absorbs more foreign gases, like nitrogen.

497
00:35:26,241 --> 00:35:30,360
If he ascends to the surface too quickly, dissolved nitrogen bubbles will turn back

498
00:35:30,361 --> 00:35:36,820
into gas, just like CO2 in a bottle of soda pop when it's uncapped.

499
00:35:36,821 --> 00:35:40,060
potentially lethal phenomenon is called the bends.

500
00:35:43,760 --> 00:35:46,760
Divers can use a computer to calculate the amount of pressure

501
00:35:46,761 --> 00:35:50,240
they're exposed to and calibrate a slow return to the surface.

502
00:35:51,860 --> 00:35:55,880
Coming back from a deep dive may take hours of slow decompression,

503
00:35:56,240 --> 00:36:00,180
so every diver knows that pressure is a force to be reckoned with.

504
00:36:03,540 --> 00:36:04,720
Pressure is everywhere.

505
00:36:04,721 --> 00:36:13,480
It's there when we need it, but when it's out of control, it can be brutally destructive.

506
00:36:13,481 --> 00:36:17,600
It can be our worst enemy underwater, but then we turn around and use it as the most

507
00:36:17,601 --> 00:36:21,280
powerful tool in a factory.

508
00:36:21,281 --> 00:36:26,140
Pressurized cans throughout your home, they not only deliver foamy lather, they can also

509
00:36:26,141 --> 00:36:30,320
dispense some tasty treats.

510
00:36:30,321 --> 00:36:33,040
Cream isn't the only food that's created with the help of pressure.

511
00:36:34,640 --> 00:36:37,920
A kernel of corn pops when the water within it turns to steam.

512
00:36:40,480 --> 00:36:43,520
Water expands 1,600 times when it becomes steam.

513
00:36:44,560 --> 00:36:48,240
In an enclosed space, that creates an incredible amount of pressure.

514
00:36:50,000 --> 00:36:55,440
The Industrial Revolution changed our world, but it can only happen after we'd harnessed steam pressure.

515
00:36:57,840 --> 00:36:59,840
So what happens if we bottle up that steam?

516
00:37:00,320 --> 00:37:03,660
and put it to work in our kitchens?

517
00:37:03,661 --> 00:37:06,520
This place has almost as much in common with a science lab

518
00:37:06,521 --> 00:37:09,020
as a kitchen.

519
00:37:09,021 --> 00:37:13,260
In fact, they call it the cooking lab.

520
00:37:13,261 --> 00:37:15,260
The team here researched and photographed

521
00:37:15,261 --> 00:37:18,780
a multi-volume book called Modernist Cuisine

522
00:37:18,781 --> 00:37:21,540
to reveal the science behind cooking

523
00:37:21,541 --> 00:37:23,680
and to discover new techniques.

524
00:37:27,740 --> 00:37:30,320
We use high-end technology to play with our food.

525
00:37:30,321 --> 00:37:32,960
for the purpose of making it more delicious, more interesting.

526
00:37:34,400 --> 00:37:37,140
Pressure is one of any kitchen's most versatile tools.

527
00:37:38,420 --> 00:37:41,600
Many homes have pressure cookers, but most of us take them for granted,

528
00:37:42,180 --> 00:37:44,120
rarely stopping to think about how they work.

529
00:37:44,980 --> 00:37:46,400
When you cook in an open pot,

530
00:37:46,880 --> 00:37:50,020
liquids can never get any hotter than 212 degrees Fahrenheit,

531
00:37:50,860 --> 00:37:52,020
the boiling point of water.

532
00:37:54,260 --> 00:37:56,180
The pressure cooker is a fascinating piece of equipment

533
00:37:56,181 --> 00:37:59,760
because it allows us to cook in a high-pressure environment

534
00:37:59,761 --> 00:38:08,000
We increased the pressure to 15 psi one bar, and that's in extremely pressurized environments.

535
00:38:08,001 --> 00:38:10,953
The pressure is now twice as great as normal.

536
00:38:10,954 --> 00:38:13,840
It's equivalent to being 30 feet underwater.

537
00:38:15,360 --> 00:38:18,331
And what's strange is that the water isn't boiling.

538
00:38:18,332 --> 00:38:20,720
It will only do that when the temperature

539
00:38:20,721 --> 00:38:26,720
surpasses 250 degrees, 38 degrees higher than the boiling point at normal pressure.

540
00:38:26,721 --> 00:38:30,720
That extra heat can brown meats or caramelize vegetables faster.

541
00:38:30,721 --> 00:38:34,720
Increasing pressure can speed up cooking, create new flavors,

542
00:38:34,721 --> 00:38:38,720
and really facilitates the cooking process in general.

543
00:38:38,721 --> 00:38:42,720
Pressure cookers have been around for more than 100 years,

544
00:38:42,721 --> 00:38:46,720
but they haven't always had safety features.

545
00:38:46,721 --> 00:38:50,720
Today, the lid is held in place by heavy flanges.

546
00:38:50,721 --> 00:38:54,720
A rubber gasket forms a hermetic seal.

547
00:38:54,721 --> 00:38:58,320
overpressurizes, a safety valve vents the excess pressure.

548
00:38:58,321 --> 00:39:00,320
It will never explode.

549
00:39:00,321 --> 00:39:03,820
It will just release all that steam and it will be safe.

550
00:39:03,821 --> 00:39:06,660
And now it's very upset.

551
00:39:06,661 --> 00:39:10,260
Steam pressure not only cooks,

552
00:39:10,261 --> 00:39:13,680
it can also be put to work stealing food or long-term storage.

553
00:39:13,681 --> 00:39:16,780
So this is a pressure canner.

554
00:39:16,781 --> 00:39:18,180
Some of the features are a little bit different

555
00:39:18,181 --> 00:39:20,220
than a traditional pressure cooker.

556
00:39:20,221 --> 00:39:24,380
A pressure canner is basically a pressure cooker

557
00:39:24,381 --> 00:39:30,495
on steroids. It can withstand almost twice as many PSI as a pressure cooker.

558
00:39:30,496 --> 00:39:31,380
Rather than

559
00:39:31,800 --> 00:39:36,400
a rubber gasket, it has a metal-to-metal seal that requires heavy clamps to bolt it down

560
00:39:36,401 --> 00:39:43,400
and hold it in place. The pressure canner also works double time. It sterilizes jars

561
00:39:44,060 --> 00:39:45,554
while sealing them.

562
00:39:45,555 --> 00:39:51,060
The process of heating and cooling the jars leaves them with a perfect

563
00:39:51,061 --> 00:39:56,060
and cooling the jars leaves them with a perfect vacuum seal.

564
00:39:56,061 --> 00:40:03,060
These pressure-canned tomatoes can survive for centuries on the shelf.

565
00:40:03,061 --> 00:40:08,060
Another pressure cooking technique was perfected to send food into space with astronauts.

566
00:40:08,061 --> 00:40:14,060
It's the opposite of cooking under pressure. This is cooking in a vacuum.

567
00:40:14,061 --> 00:40:21,060
Flavor, texture and freshness are all preserved, as water is drawn out under less than a thousandth of a pound of PSI.

568
00:40:21,061 --> 00:40:26,060
This is what's called freeze drying.

569
00:40:26,061 --> 00:40:29,060
These carrots are very light.

570
00:40:29,061 --> 00:40:34,060
Only 1 to 4% of the water that was already in them

571
00:40:34,061 --> 00:40:35,060
is still there.

572
00:40:35,061 --> 00:40:38,060
So it's very, very, very dry.

573
00:40:38,061 --> 00:40:41,060
Once reconstituted, these freeze dried veggies

574
00:40:41,061 --> 00:40:44,060
look like something you'd find in a cup of dry noodle soup

575
00:40:44,061 --> 00:40:45,060
or ramen.

576
00:40:45,061 --> 00:40:47,060
So with freeze drying, we're manipulating pressure

577
00:40:47,061 --> 00:40:50,060
to investigate the future of cooking.

578
00:40:50,061 --> 00:40:58,920
Lightweight, dehydrated foods are perfect for outer space, where every ounce of launch

579
00:40:58,921 --> 00:41:02,340
cargo is carefully managed.

580
00:41:02,341 --> 00:41:07,500
In fact, one dish, freeze-dried ice cream, was invented for NASA and flew on an Apollo

581
00:41:07,501 --> 00:41:11,440
space mission in the 1960s.

582
00:41:11,441 --> 00:41:16,700
And it's all thanks to the incredibly versatile force we call pressure.

583
00:41:16,701 --> 00:41:30,260
We use it to build and destroy, to rescue, to restore, and to explore.

584
00:41:30,261 --> 00:41:36,080
Wherever you go and whatever you do, pressure will be right there with you.

